    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey i'm looking at some ceramic fragments from a late helladic layer and i'm having trouble telling if the surface treatment is a slip or a true glaze. are there specific indicators in the firing process or the shard texture that can help me differentiate the two?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To distinguish between slip and glaze, examine the shard's temperature markings: slips usually yield rough, matte surfaces that become shiny and polished only with calcination at high temperatures (776°C), whereas true glazes have distinct chemical markings on the clay body that are easily visible without extreme heat. You can also look for shrinkage gaps or needle friction lines near the surface, which are characteristic of slips but generally absent in well-fired glazes. Ultimately, the absence of these surface treatment marks suggests the layer is unglazed, while their presence indicates slip or glaze application.",
